The tsconfig has `strict: true` enabled, which means noImplicitAny, strictNullChecks, etc. are all on. The file compiles cleanly under strict mode.

Let me double-check one thing - the `isServerMessage` function. With strict mode, `'type' in data` works because `data` has been narrowed to `object` (since `typeof data === 'object' && data !== null`). So `'type' in data` is valid for the type `object`. This compiles fine.

Everything looks good. Let me also verify that the file is complete and doesn't have any issues.

Actually, let me re-read the task more carefully:

> Export these types (matching T02 exactly):
> 1. PhaseDescriptor = { id: string; label: string; icon: string }
> 2. LogEntry = { id: string; timestamp: string; type: 'text'|'thinking'|'tool_call'|'tool_call_start'|'tool_call_end'|'decision'|'error'; content: string; metadata?: Record<string, unknown> }
> 3. AgentWindowState = { agentId: string; profile: string; taskId?: string; active: boolean; log: LogEntry[] }
> 4. SidebarInfo = { title: string; indicator: string; phases?: PhaseDescriptor[] }
> 5. WorkflowSummary = { id: string; workflowName: string; status: 'running'|'completed'|'failed'; sidebar: SidebarInfo; startedAt: string; completedAt?: string }
> 6. ServerMessage — same discriminated union as T02 (init, workflow_started, workflow_sidebar, workflow_phase, workflow_complete, workflow_failed, agent_spawned, agent_log, agent_complete)
> 7. ClientMessage — same as T02

The task says "Export these types (matching T02 exactly)" - it uses `=` syntax like a type alias. The implementation uses `interface`. Both are semantically equivalent for these object shapes. In TypeScript, `interface` vs `type` for object types is mostly interchangeable. This is a stylistic choice, not a correctness issue. The task spec writes them with `=` implying type aliases, but interfaces are generally preferred in TypeScript for public API surfaces, especially when they represent data structures. This is not a problem.

> Additional frontend-specific types:
> 8. WorkflowRunState = { summary: WorkflowSummary; agents: Map<string, AgentWindowState>; currentPhase: string; completedPhases: string[] }
> 9. AppGlobalState = { workflows: WorkflowSummary[]; selectedRunId: string | null; runStates: Map<string, WorkflowRunState> }

